{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":268506,"name":"","username":"RepoRover","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.rubyonrails.org/reporover/{size}/14574_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-14T18:16:10.350Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI’m trying to understand how teams check a Rails migration before it reaches a production PostgreSQL database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat do you look at in review: the migration file, the diff of \u003ccode\u003eschema.rb\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003estructure.sql\u003c/code\u003e, or the SQL it runs?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat has bitten you in production: a \u003ccode\u003erename_column\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003eremove_column\u003c/code\u003e under running code, an \u003ccode\u003eadd_index\u003c/code\u003e or \u003ccode\u003echange_column\u003c/code\u003e that locked a large table, a new \u003ccode\u003enull: false\u003c/code\u003e on rows that already existed? What happened, and what did it take to put right?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDo you run migrations against a copy of production or a staging database first, and do you test the \u003ccode\u003edown\u003c/code\u003e?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eDo you use a checker (strong_migrations, something home-made), and what does it still miss?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’m an engineer working on tooling in this area, and I’d rather learn from what happens in real projects than guess. Thank you.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2026-11-14T18:16:10.350Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":10,"readers_count":9,"score":6.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":91984,"topic_slug":"how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production","display_username":"","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":29793,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production/91984/1","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":268507,"name":"Lorin Thwaits","username":"Lorin_Thwaits","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.rubyonrails.org/lorin_thwaits/{size}/9491_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-14T18:50:48.724Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI would always test locally with a representative copy of some rows from production (changed only to have anonymisation), and confirm that both up and down work well.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThen see the up work OK in staging, and promote to production.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2026-11-14T18:50:48.724Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":1,"reads":10,"readers_count":9,"score":6.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":91984,"topic_slug":"how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production","display_username":"Lorin Thwaits","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":10471,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production/91984/2","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":268508,"name":"","username":"RepoRover","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.rubyonrails.org/reporover/{size}/14574_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-14T19:09:54.311Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThank you, that is a clear routine. Just two follow-ups if you have a minute:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003col\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eHas the copy of production rows ever caught something that an empty database would have let through? I’d like to hear what it was.\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eHow do you get that copy? A script that anonymises a sample, or by hand? And does every developer on the team have it, or mostly you?\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ol\u003e","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2026-11-14T19:09:54.311Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":2,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":9,"readers_count":8,"score":1.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":91984,"topic_slug":"how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production","display_username":"","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":10471,"username":"Lorin_Thwaits","name":"Lorin Thwaits","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.rubyonrails.org/lorin_thwaits/{size}/9491_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":29793,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production/91984/3","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":268511,"name":"Saurabh Khatri","username":"SikhamiLearn","avatar_template":"https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/s/8491ac/{size}.png","created_at":"2026-11-15T07:43:06.905Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eA few things that have helped in practice: run the migration against a restored, recent copy of production (with personal data scrubbed) rather than a small sample, because lock and timing problems only show up at real table sizes. Time it there, and if it takes more than a few seconds, split it. The strong_migrations gem catches most of the dangerous patterns at development time, such as adding an index without `algorithm: :concurrently` or changing a column type in place. For column changes, the safe route is usually to add the new column, backfill in batches, switch reads, and drop the old column in a later deployment. Setting a `lock_timeout` in the migration also means it fails fast instead of blocking the table if it can’t get a lock.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":4,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2026-11-15T07:43:06.905Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":8,"readers_count":7,"score":6.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":91984,"topic_slug":"how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production","display_username":"Saurabh Khatri","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":29748,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production/91984/4","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":268517,"name":"","username":"RepoRover","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.rubyonrails.org/reporover/{size}/14574_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-16T19:34:58.353Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThank you, this is practical. Has the restored copy of production ever caught a lock or timing problem that strong_migrations had let through? I’d like to hear what it was. And how do you keep that copy fresh: a scheduled restore, or by hand when needed?\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2026-11-16T19:34:58.353Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":4,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":6,"readers_count":5,"score":1.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":91984,"topic_slug":"how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production","display_username":"","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":29748,"username":"SikhamiLearn","name":"Saurabh Khatri","avatar_template":"https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/s/8491ac/{size}.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":29793,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production/91984/5","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":268518,"name":"Saurabh Khatri","username":"SikhamiLearn","avatar_template":"https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/s/8491ac/{size}.png","created_at":"2026-11-17T07:40:25.329Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eGlad it helps, \u003ca class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/reporover\"\u003e@RepoRover\u003c/a\u003e. I can’t point to one specific incident of my own, so I’d rather describe what a full-size copy typically shows than invent a story. The usual catches are a backfill or index build that takes minutes on real data but milliseconds on a small sample, and a null: false change that fails on old rows nobody expected to be null. Those only appear with realistic volume and messy legacy data.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor freshness, a scheduled restore from the latest backup, weekly or nightly, works well. It also proves your backups restore. If it is manual, it tends to get stale and people stop trusting it.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":6,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2026-11-17T07:40:25.329Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":5,"readers_count":4,"score":6.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":91984,"topic_slug":"how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production","display_username":"Saurabh Khatri","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":29748,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production/91984/6","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":268523,"name":"","username":"RepoRover","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.rubyonrails.org/reporover/{size}/14574_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-17T19:16:45.211Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThank you for saying it straight. The point that a scheduled restore also proves the backups work, and that a manual one goes stale, is one I will keep.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":7,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2026-11-17T19:16:45.211Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":6,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":5,"readers_count":4,"score":1.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":91984,"topic_slug":"how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production","display_username":"","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":29748,"username":"SikhamiLearn","name":"Saurabh Khatri","avatar_template":"https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/s/8491ac/{size}.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":29793,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production/91984/7","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":268529,"name":"Muhammad Mudassir","username":"thequranclass","avatar_template":"https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/t/a6a055/{size}.png","created_at":"2026-11-20T12:04:51.721Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThis post was flagged by the community and is temporarily hidden.\u003c/p\u003e","cooked_hidden":true,"post_number":8,"post_type":1,"posts_count":9,"updated_at":"2026-11-20T12:04:53.669Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":5,"readers_count":4,"score":0.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":91984,"topic_slug":"how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production","display_username":"Muhammad Mudassir","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":29864,"hidden":true,"hidden_reason_id":1,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":false,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/how-do-you-check-a-migration-before-it-runs-in-production/91984/8","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":268542,"name":"","username":"RepoRover","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.rubyonrails.org/reporover/{size}/14574_2.png","created_at":"2026-11-22T20:34:04.589Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThank you, Lorin and SikhamiLearn, this answered my question. What I take from it: run the migration against a recent, scrubbed copy of production and time it, because lock and timing problems only show at 